ure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-86848" :question/id #uuid "9a2a764e-9e6f-5cbb-9a5b-315357d501db" :question/math-academy-id "q-86848" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}]},\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 5+\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 0}{(-1)^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=\\frac{5}{2},$ $b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{5}{2})}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-86848/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e43d27-ffaf-58ea-bb6e-664b70770003"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-86848"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-40738"] :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=2,$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-41032/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer/id #uuid "73be5941-3990-54c7-a242-