],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{5\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=3,$ $b=2,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-41032/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e43d27-ffaf-58ea-bb6e-664b70770003"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-41032"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-86845/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" :answer/id #uuid "1339361e-d70e-59d2-9029-dca83fba6545" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate