\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{c}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot 1}{(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $b=5,$ $c=-2,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{b}=\\frac{-2}{5}=-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-86845/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e43d27-ffaf-58ea-bb6e-664b70770003"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-86845"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-81211/selection/75be7144d0f06979df25a4d833026b673a3f8e67075b82357bb7a90b42abaa01" :answer/id #uuid "cea44f8f-81ee-5420-b9b1-92dfacefd3d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81211/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4