& 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 3}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $b=2,$ $c=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}=-4.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140529/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15719b6-0ce4-5fb8-80be-abcaf9344a10"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140529"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-40741"] :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 0}{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{1}{2},$ $b=\\frac{3}{2},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-86858/selection/a05724295bc0340162295da21b8749cd336584b7c76ed9f6810ddb03e7f98d00" :answer/id #uuid "a5b62e9a-6552-5681-a13a-e9